--- ANSWER ---
The claim is right about the numbers but wrong to say "only because" — it hides a second, real failed prediction. The read count of 47 does follow from comparing against the s09 file plus two new items, and both new items do map to TC2 and #-24. But the prediction of 48 failed too. The cause is a defect in the predictor rule, not a one-off mistake in the prediction, and the claim waves it away as "the pred's error".

## 1. Strongest reason the claim is wrong
**The missing Obtain item comes from the predictor rule, and the rule will be wrong again.** In `tools/elrule.py:153-158`, rule A counts a created primitive as an error item if it has *any* unwired sink. Only SubVIs are filtered by required terminals (`elrule.py:136-152`, from `subvi_requiredness.json`). Primitives have no such filter.

The prediction's own detail (`plan_ring_p5s01a_pred.json:222-230`) shows why #-3 was counted. Its four unwired sinks are all optional: `name (unnamed)`, `create if not found? (T)`, `error in (no error)` and `max queue size (-1, unlimited)`. In LabVIEW naming, a default shown in brackets marks an optional input. NI's material describes `element data type` as the input that sets the queue's type, and `max queue size` as defaulting to unlimited ([NI KB, queues](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z000000P7OfSAK), [labviewwiki Queue Operations](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Functions_Palette/Programming/Synchronization/Queue_Operations)). I could not fetch NI's own Obtain Queue reference page, so the required/optional status of each input is **not confirmed from the primary source**.

Rule A got #-24 (Enqueue) right only because `queue` (required) happens to be unwired alongside its optional inputs. It got #-56 right only by luck: TC2's empty False-frame inner row was counted as an "input" item, but the real item is in a class the rule does not know, "Tunnel: Missing assignment" ([NI docs](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview/page/failing-to-wire-a-tunnel-in-all-cases-of-a-structure.html)). So the 48 vs 47 gap is one over-count, and one class error that happened to give the right count. The docstring even says "an optional input gives no item" (`elrule.py:30-31`), but that is applied only to base nodes. Any later P5 session that creates a Dequeue, Release Queue or similar primitive with optional inputs left unwired will over-predict in the same way.

## 2. Alternative explanations of the same evidence
- **(a) Hidden swaps among wire items.** "missing 0" matches items by text. The 22 "not connected" and 21 "loose ends" items all have identical text (log lines 34-76), so a base wire item closing while a new one opens cannot be seen. Class counts that equal the base's do not show those 43 items are the same ones.
  - The scratch's "lost 1 == sim 1" (wire `30457`, `diag_c154_p5s01a_scratch.log:233`) is predicted. So this alternative is weak, but the Error List cannot rule it out.
- **(b) Obtain's wire not landing.** I rejected this. If `w_qk_obt` had not landed, Obtain's required input would be unwired and an item would appear. Its absence supports the claim, and E1 (real graph == simulated graph at every checkpoint, `scratch.log:230`) agrees.
- **(c) The Tunnel item being some other tunnel.** I rejected this as well. The census adds 3 SelectorTunnel + 1 Tunnel with 6 outer and 10 inner terminals (`scratch.log:235`). That fits exactly 3 case tunnels, the case selector and one shift-register pair, so TC2 is the only new case *output* tunnel. TD PASS (`:238`) rules out a base tunnel losing its wire.

## 3. What would falsify the claim
In the P5-1a graph, any of these would falsify it:
- Obtain's `element data type` row has no wire.
- Enqueue's `element` is unwired.
- TC2's False-frame inner row is wired.
- A second case output tunnel exists with an unwired frame.

Separately, the predictor diagnosis is falsified if `elrule.predict` still gives 48 after rule A is limited to required primitive terminals.

## 4. Cheapest test that separates the claim from the alternatives
This is offline, on the graph json that step 6 of brief 154-a3-3 produces:
1. Check `wire_uid` on the rows of Obtain (5 sinks), Enqueue (`queue`, `element`) and TC2 (both inner rows). The claim predicts that only Obtain's `element data type`, Enqueue's `element` and TC2's True-frame row are wired.
2. Re-run `elrule.predict` on the P5-1a step files with a list of required terminals for primitives (Obtain: `element data type`; Enqueue: `queue`, `element`). It should give 47, with TC2 classed separately as "tunnel".

If both hold, the claim stands on its facts, and only the predictor needs fixing. One caveat for whoever acts on this: the fix is in `tools/elrule.py`, which brief 154-a3-3 pins as unchanged during the card (step 8 invariants), so it belongs to a later card.

The by-design check (`elrule.py:238-239`) only accepts Local / While-condition / shift-register items. It will therefore report STOP on every session that adds an input or tunnel item. This MISMATCH is structural and will repeat; it is not a one-off.

